WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.538
Logic games offer a chance to make strategy visible.

2
00:00:03.738 --> 00:00:12.054
Before a move in Tangram Packing, Flow Free, Lights Out, Flood Fill, Untangle or Dots and Boxes, ask the learner to

3
00:00:12.254 --> 00:00:14.035
predict what will happen.

4
00:00:14.235 --> 00:00:17.777
After testing it, ask what changed in the strategy.

5
00:00:17.977 --> 00:00:20.826
The explanation is the instructional evidence.

6
00:00:21.026 --> 00:00:26.624
A high score by itself does not show why the learner succeeded or whether the idea transfers.

7
00:00:26.824 --> 00:00:30.396
SplatBot Studio adds a programming-style sequence.

8
00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:35.330
Have students predict the result of a short command sequence before running it.

9
00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:40.652
When the result differs from the goal, change one part and explain the reason.

10
00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:45.220
Keep the initial task small enough that learners can inspect the steps.

11
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.549
A paper sequence with arrows can provide a useful alternative and a transfer check.

12
00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:55.351
Preview themes, reading demands and controls for your students.

13
00:00:55.551 --> 00:01:00.663
Some games use conflict or traffic themes, while others are calmer puzzles.

14
00:01:00.863 --> 00:01:03.718
Fun Quiz packs need a content review.

15
00:01:03.918 --> 00:01:10.129
Arcade games may simply serve recreation; do not attach an unsupported subject-learning claim.

16
00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:15.705
Pause and write a debrief question and a fresh off-screen problem for a game you might use.
